-- title:
--   Function field of a Čerednik–Drinfeld quotient embeds into invariant functions
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $r$ and the following data.
--
--   **Base ring.** A characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), an irreducible element $\pi \in \mathcal O$, with $\mathcal O$ $\pi$-adically complete (`hcomplete`), residue ring of cardinality $r$ (`hres`), and $(r) = (\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$ (`hunr`, absolute unramifiedness); a characteristic-zero field $K_0$ which is a fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **Unramified coefficients.** A characteristic-zero domain $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ over $\mathcal O$ together with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, subject to: $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every element of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $\mathrm{Fr}(x) \equiv x^r \pmod \pi$ for all $x$ (`hFr`).
--
--   **Valuation of the determinant.** A homomorphism $v\!\det : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) characterised by `hvdet`: $v\!\det(g) = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\pi^n$ in $K_0$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **Group data.** A group $G$, a homomorphism $\sigma : G \to \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ and a subgroup $\Gamma \le G$ such that some $z \in \Gamma$ has $\sigma z$ a scalar matrix $c \cdot 1$ with $v\!\det(\sigma z) = 2$ (`hcent`) and some $w \in \Gamma$ has $v\!\det(\sigma w) = 1$ (`hodd`); a subgroup $\Gamma'$ whose members are exactly the $x \in \Gamma$ with $v\!\det(\sigma x)$ even (`hΓ'`). A homomorphism $\rho : G \to \mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ given on each $g$ by the class of $\sigma g$ (`hρ`), such that for every vertex $v$ of the Bruhat–Tits tree (a homothety class of full $\mathcal O$-lattices in $K_0^2$) the stabiliser of $v$ in $\rho(\Gamma')$ is finite (`hdisc`), and such that finitely many vertices meet every $\rho(\Gamma')$-orbit (`hcocpt`).
--
--   **The scheme.** A scheme $\mathcal X$ with a proper flat morphism $f : \mathcal X \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, whose generic fibre is smooth of relative dimension $1$: the second projection of the pullback of $f$ along $\operatorname{Spec}(K_0) \to \operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal O)$ satisfies `SmoothOfRelativeDimension 1` (`hsmooth`).
--
--   **The uniformising transformation $\Theta$.** For every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, a map
--   $$\Theta_B : (O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to_{\mathcal O} B) \times \mathrm{DeligneDatum}(\pi, B) \longrightarrow \{\varphi : \operatorname{Spec} B \to \mathcal X \mid \varphi \text{ followed by } f = \operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal O \to B)\},$$
--   where a Deligne datum over $B$ assigns to each full lattice $M$ a $B$-submodule $\mathrm{line}(M)$ of $B \otimes_{\mathcal O} M$ with invertible quotient, monotone for inclusions of lattices, equivariant for scalar homotheties, and nondegenerate at every prime of $B$ in the sense spelled out in `DeligneDatum`. The hypotheses on $\Theta$ are: naturality in $B$ for $\mathcal O$-algebra maps (`hΘnat`); invariance under the twisted $\Gamma$-action (`hΘinv`), i.e. $\Theta_B x' = \Theta_B x$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma$ and `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` holds for $\sigma\gamma$, which means that the $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-component of $x'$ is that of $x$ composed with $\mathrm{Fr}^{-v\!\det(\sigma\gamma)}$ and the Deligne datum of $x'$ is the pullback of that of $x$ along $(\sigma\gamma)^{-1}$; the fibre description over algebraically closed fields (`hΘfib`), i.e. for every algebraically closed field $k$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra with $\pi$ nilpotent and every $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to_{\mathcal O} k$, the map $P \mapsto \Theta_k(\psi, P)$ is surjective onto the $k$-points of $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and $\Theta_k(\psi,P) = \Theta_k(\psi,P')$ holds precisely when $(\psi,P)$ and $(\psi,P')$ are related by the twisted action of some $\gamma \in \Gamma$; and universality (`hΘuniv`), i.e. for every scheme $T$ with $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and every family $\rho'$ of maps into the nilpotent-$\pi$ points of $t$ which is natural in $B$ and invariant under the twisted $\Gamma$-action, there is a natural family $u$ from the nilpotent-$\pi$ points of $f$ to those of $t$ with $u \circ \Theta = \rho'$, and any natural $u'$ with $u' \circ \Theta = \rho'$ coincides with $u$ at every $B$ and every point.
--
--   **The analytic side.** A field $C$ over $K_0$, algebraically closed and complete for a valuation with values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$; a commutative ring $R$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra with $C$ an $R$-algebra, compatibly with the towers $\mathcal O \to R \to C$ and $\mathcal O \to K_0 \to C$; a pseudo-uniformiser $\varpi$, that is an element of $K_0$ whose value in $C$ lies strictly between $0$ and $1$ and such that every nonzero $a \in K_0$ satisfies $v(\varpi)^N \le v(a) \le v(\varpi)^{-N}$ for some $N$; the frame hypothesis `hF`, asserting that $\pi$ is irreducible, $R \to C$ is injective with image exactly $\{c \in C : v(c) \le 1\}$, $R$ is $\pi$-adically complete, $\mathcal O$ is exactly the set of $a \in K_0$ with $v(a) \le 1$, the image of $K_0$ in $C$ is closed, and $\pi$ and $\varpi$ have the same image in $C$; an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi_0 : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to R$; the rank-one condition `hrk` (for $x$ with $v(x) < 1$ and $y \ne 0$ there is $n$ with $v(x)^n \le v(y)$) and the cofinality condition `hval` (every nonzero $\varepsilon \in \Gamma_0$ dominates $v(y)$ for some $y \ne 0$); the exhaustion hypothesis `hex`, that every point of the Drinfeld upper half plane $\Omega = C \setminus K_0$ lies in one of the affinoids $\mathrm{affinoid}\,\varpi\,n$; and the hypothesis that `Omega.HolRingOf ϖ ρ` is a domain, this type being by definition the subring `holRing ϖ` of functions $\Omega \to C$ whose restriction to each affinoid is a uniform limit of a uniformly bounded sequence of quotients of polynomials without poles there.
--
--   **The uniformisation of points.** A map $\Phi$ from adic points, i.e. compatible systems $(x_n)$ of Deligne data over $R/(\pi^{n+1})$, to the $R$-points of $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, subject to the four-fold hypothesis `hΦ`: (i) for every $x$ and $n$, the reduction of $\Phi x$ modulo $\pi^{n+1}$ equals $\Theta$ evaluated at the pair consisting of $\psi_0$ reduced modulo $\pi^{n+1}$ and $x_n$; (ii) $\Phi$ is surjective; (iii) $\Phi x = \Phi x'$ if and only if $x' = x \cdot \sigma\gamma$ for some $\gamma \in \Gamma'$; (iv) base change along $R \to C$ is injective on $R$-points of $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec} R$, and every $C$-point of $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec} C$ is the base change of an $R$-point.
--
--   Finally, a morphism $s_C : \operatorname{Spec} C \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ equal to the spectrum of the composite $\mathcal O \to K_0 \to C$ (`hsC`), and the assumption that the pullback $\mathcal X_C$ of $f$ along $s_C$ is integral.
--
--   **Conclusion.** There exists a ring homomorphism
--   $$e : K(\mathcal X_C) \longrightarrow \mathrm{Mumford.invariantFieldOf}\ C\ G\ (\mathrm{HolRingOf}\ \varpi\ \rho)\ \Gamma',$$
--   from the function field of $\mathcal X_C$ to the subfield of the fraction field of $\mathrm{HolRingOf}\,\varpi\,\rho$ consisting of the elements fixed by $\gamma \bullet$ for every $\gamma \in \Gamma'$ (for the $G$-action carried by that fraction field), such that both of the following hold.
--
--   First, $e$ is $C$-linear: for every $c \in C$, the image under $e$ of `baseToFunctionField` of the structure morphism $\mathcal X_C \to \operatorname{Spec} C$ applied to $c$ — that is, of the germ at the generic point of the global section of $\mathcal X_C$ determined by $c$ — equals the image of $c$ under the structure map of $C$ into the invariant field.
--
--   Second, $e$ is compatible with evaluation along $\Phi$: for every open $U \subseteq \mathcal X_C$ containing the generic point and every section $s \in \mathcal O_{\mathcal X_C}(U)$ there are $a, b \in \mathrm{HolRingOf}\,\varpi\,\rho$ with $b$ a non-zero-divisor such that (i) the image of $e$ applied to the germ of $s$ at the generic point, viewed in the fraction field, is the class $a/b$; and (ii) for every adic point $x$, every $z$ in the upper half plane $\Omega$ with $z$ equal to $x.\mathrm{toOmega}\,C$ (the unique scalar, if it exists, with $(z,1)$ spanning the $C$-line attached to $x$, and $0$ otherwise), and every morphism $q : \operatorname{Spec} C \to \mathcal X_C$ whose composite with the first projection is the base change to $C$ of $\Phi x$ and whose composite with the second projection is the identity of $\operatorname{Spec} C$, and for every witness that $q^{-1}(U)$ is the whole of $\operatorname{Spec} C$, one has
--   $$b(z) \cdot \bigl(q^{*}s\bigr) = a(z)$$
--   in $C$, where $a$ and $b$ are regarded as functions on $\Omega$ and $q^{*}s$ denotes the global section of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ obtained by pulling $s$ back along $q$, restricted to the top open and identified with an element of $C$ through the isomorphism $\Gamma(\operatorname{Spec} C, \mathcal O) \cong C$.
--
--   This is the comparison step of the Čerednik–Drinfeld construction which converts the uniformisation of points, $\Phi$, into a map of function fields: rational functions on the generic fibre $\mathcal X_C$ become $\Gamma'$-invariant meromorphic functions on the Drinfeld upper half plane, with the compatibility recorded as an explicit evaluation identity $b(z)\, s(\Phi x) = a(z)$ at each adic point. It is used by the statement producing a ring isomorphism between the function field of the pullback and the invariant field in the presence of tameness and virtual torsion-freeness, and its proof combines the chartwise-meromorphic construction of the embedding with the finiteness, clearing-of-denominators and holomorphy statements for the same quotient datum.
